The site's first change to the scene of "White Room, Pixel Traced", applied by
scripts/vendor-whiteroom.sh after it copies the scene out of the zip.

The page frames the room at 3:2; the site renders it full width at half the
window height, from about 0.9:1 to 12:1 and wider (whiteroom.ts keeps the
vertical field of view and widens the horizontal one). Wide frames see past
the left end of the back wall and floor at x = -1400; tall frames see over
the walls at 470 cm. So the back wall, its window band and the floor run on
to x = -5000 (enough for about 20:1), and the walls rise to 620 cm (more than
the tallest frame, capped at 37 degrees half-angle, reaches). No ceiling or
left wall: the room is lit mostly by the open sky they would shut out. The
window's light source grows with it, so the far wall is lit as the near one.

Where the walls meet the floor, and each other in the corner, a small cove
(3 cm) curves one into the other, painted like them (MAT.WALL, so it follows
the theme as they do). Without it the walls met the glossy floor in a harsh,
razor-straight line no real room has; with it the junction still reads, as a
soft shading change a few pixels wide rather than a one-pixel edge. (A
skirting did that before, but its top made a visible edge of its own.) The
cove is a primitive kind of the site's own (KIND.COVE, the inside of a
cylinder between its axis and the corner), which whiteroom.ts teaches the
room's shader; it meets wall and floor tangentially, so it has no edge.

